   "lineHeight": 1.25,
   "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P1 follow-up: the browser lease becomes run-scoped and lease-keyed; the\nlifecycle fixes around it).\n\nclients/bridge/src/browser/profile.ts\n  ProfileManager gains the RUN scope on top of the lease primitive:\n    withRunLease({leaseId, profileId, session}) - one page and one cookie jar per LEASE, taken on the\n      first step that names it and handed back unchanged to every later step of the same lease.\n      `acquire()` stays as the primitive underneath it and still serialises per profileId.\n    touchRun(leaseId, {profileId}) - the keepalive: re-arms the idle backstop, never creates a lease.\n    releaseRun(leaseId, {reason, profileId}) - the teardown: localStorage, then the page, then the\n      jar. THROWS when the wipe fails; the executor turns that into a failed step on the wire.\n    openRuns() - live leases, for the status surface and tests.\n  The lifecycle verbs resolve a profile FIRST, like every page verb, so a frame cannot end a lease on\n  another owner's profile. A second LEASE on one profile queues for the duration of the first - one\n  browser, one jar, one Chromium singleton - but a sub-automation is not a second lease.\n\n  THE LIFECYCLE FIXES landing with it, all in the same file:\n   - The stale-SingletonLock recovery used `existsSync`, which FOLLOWS symlinks. Real Chrome writes\n     SingletonLock as a symlink to `<hostname>-<pid>`, and after a crash that target does not exist -\n     so existsSync returned false for exactly the dangling lock the recovery exists to clear, and the\n     recovery was dead code against the browser it was written for. Now `lstatSync`, which does not\n     follow. The sweep still never runs while this process holds a context for the profile, and that\n     guard - unreachable through the public API - is pinned by a test that calls the method directly.\n   - Idle-close deleted the `held` entry BEFORE `context.close()` resolved, so the map said \"nothing\n     here\" while Chromium was still shutting down: the next acquire swept the singleton markers of a\n     live browser and launched into the collision. The entry now stays, marked `closing`, and\n     `ensureContext` awaits that close before it sweeps and relaunches.\n   - `acquire` checked the `closed` flag at its top and then awaited the per-profile chain, which can\n     be a whole run long; `ensureContext` never re-checked. A waiter that woke after `closeAll`\n     launched a fresh HEADED browser into a daemon that was shutting down. Shutdown is now final: the\n     check moved into `ensureContext`, and a waiter that wakes after close is refused.\n   - `lease.page()` after release used to open a fresh page nobody would ever close (a step still in\n     flight when the run's release lands). It now refuses by name.\n\nclients/bridge/src/cli/commands/serve.ts\n  installShutdown() - the SIGINT/SIGTERM sequence as ONE testable unit (signal registration injected,\n  so a test never raises a real signal): zeroize secrets, close the socket and surface, AWAIT the\n  browser teardown bounded at 10s, then drop the pidfile and resolve. It used to be a closure inside\n  `serve()` whose ordering nothing could assert, which is how `void profiles.closeAll()` survived.\n\napi/src/automation/browser-session.ts\n  BrowserLease {id, used} - one per call tree, minted per execution PASS, threaded through\n  `RunContext.browserLease` so a sub-automation is a TENANT of its parent's lease rather than a\n  second one queueing behind it. `used` is set by the session on its first frame (not at\n  construction: the engine builds a session for every step of a daemon-connected run).\n  releaseBrowserLease() - the run-end signal, sent once by the owning pass from the engine's run\n  `finally`. Never throws, never silent.\n  DaemonBrowserSession - stamps the leaseId on every frame, heartbeats the lease every 45s from its\n  first step, and stops the heartbeat on dispose(). dispose() no longer ends the lease: a\n  sub-automation's session disposing would have torn the browser down under its parent.",
   "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P1 follow-up: the browser lease becomes run-scoped and lease-keyed; the\nlifecycle fixes around it).\n\nclients/bridge/src/browser/profile.ts\n  ProfileManager gains the RUN scope on top of the lease primitive:\n    withRunLease({leaseId, profileId, session}) - one page and one cookie jar per LEASE, taken on the\n      first step that names it and handed back unchanged to every later step of the same lease.\n      `acquire()` stays as the primitive underneath it and still serialises per profileId.\n    touchRun(leaseId, {profileId}) - the keepalive: re-arms the idle backstop, never creates a lease.\n    releaseRun(leaseId, {reason, profileId}) - the teardown: localStorage, then the page, then the\n      jar. THROWS when the wipe fails; the executor turns that into a failed step on the wire.\n    openRuns() - live leases, for the status surface and tests.\n  The lifecycle verbs resolve a profile FIRST, like every page verb, so a frame cannot end a lease on\n  another owner's profile. A second LEASE on one profile queues for the duration of the first - one\n  browser, one jar, one Chromium singleton - but a sub-automation is not a second lease.\n\n  THE LIFECYCLE FIXES landing with it, all in the same file:\n   - The stale-SingletonLock recovery used `existsSync`, which FOLLOWS symlinks. Real Chrome writes\n     SingletonLock as a symlink to `<hostname>-<pid>`, and after a crash that target does not exist -\n     so existsSync returned false for exactly the dangling lock the recovery exists to clear, and the\n     recovery was dead code against the browser it was written for. Now `lstatSync`, which does not\n     follow. The sweep still never runs while this process holds a context for the profile, and that\n     guard - unreachable through the public API - is pinned by a test that calls the method directly.\n   - Idle-close deleted the `held` entry BEFORE `context.close()` resolved, so the map said \"nothing\n     here\" while Chromium was still shutting down: the next acquire swept the singleton markers of a\n     live browser and launched into the collision. The entry now stays, marked `closing`, and\n     `ensureContext` awaits that close before it sweeps and relaunches.\n   - `acquire` checked the `closed` flag at its top and then awaited the per-profile chain, which can\n     be a whole run long; `ensureContext` never re-checked. A waiter that woke after `closeAll`\n     launched a fresh HEADED browser into a daemon that was shutting down. Shutdown is now final: the\n     check moved into `ensureContext`, and a waiter that wakes after close is refused.\n   - `lease.page()` after release used to open a fresh page nobody would ever close (a step still in\n     flight when the run's release lands). It now refuses by name.\n\nclients/bridge/src/cli/commands/serve.ts\n  installShutdown() - the SIGINT/SIGTERM sequence as ONE testable unit (signal registration injected,\n  so a test never raises a real signal): zeroize secrets, close the socket and surface, AWAIT the\n  browser teardown bounded at 10s, then drop the pidfile and resolve. It used to be a closure inside\n  `serve()` whose ordering nothing could assert, which is how `void profiles.closeAll()` survived.\n\napi/src/automation/browser-session.ts\n  BrowserLease {id, used} - one per call tree, minted per execution PASS, threaded through\n  `RunContext.browserLease` so a sub-automation is a TENANT of its parent's lease rather than a\n  second one queueing behind it. `used` is set by the session on its first frame (not at\n  construction: the engine builds a session for every step of a daemon-connected run).\n  releaseBrowserLease() - the run-end signal, sent once by the owning pass from the engine's run\n  `finally`. Never throws, never silent.\n  DaemonBrowserSession - stamps the leaseId on every frame, heartbeats the lease every 45s from its\n  first step, and stops the heartbeat on dispose(). dispose() no longer ends the lease: a\n  sub-automation's session disposing would have torn the browser down under its parent.",
   "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P1 follow-up: the browser lease becomes run-scoped and lease-keyed; the\nlifecycle fixes around it).\n\nclients/bridge/src/browser/profile.ts\n  ProfileManager gains the RUN scope on top of the lease primitive:\n    withRunLease({leaseId, profileId, session}) - one page and one cookie jar per LEASE, taken on the\n      first step that names it and handed back unchanged to every later step of the same lease.\n      `acquire()` stays as the primitive underneath it and still serialises per profileId.\n    touchRun(leaseId, {profileId}) - the keepalive: re-arms the idle backstop, never creates a lease.\n    releaseRun(leaseId, {reason, profileId}) - the teardown: localStorage, then the page, then the\n      jar. THROWS when the wipe fails; the executor turns that into a failed step on the wire.\n    openRuns() - live leases, for the status surface and tests.\n  The lifecycle verbs resolve a profile FIRST, like every page verb, so a frame cannot end a lease on\n  another owner's profile. A second LEASE on one profile queues for the duration of the first - one\n  browser, one jar, one Chromium singleton - but a sub-automation is not a second lease.\n\n  THE LIFECYCLE FIXES landing with it, all in the same file:\n   - The stale-SingletonLock recovery used `existsSync`, which FOLLOWS symlinks. Real Chrome writes\n     SingletonLock as a symlink to `<hostname>-<pid>`, and after a crash that target does not exist -\n     so existsSync returned false for exactly the dangling lock the recovery exists to clear, and the\n     recovery was dead code against the browser it was written for. Now `lstatSync`, which does not\n     follow. The sweep still never runs while this process holds a context for the profile, and that\n     guard - unreachable through the public API - is pinned by a test that calls the method directly.\n   - Idle-close deleted the `held` entry BEFORE `context.close()` resolved, so the map said \"nothing\n     here\" while Chromium was still shutting down: the next acquire swept the singleton markers of a\n     live browser and launched into the collision. The entry now stays, marked `closing`, and\n     `ensureContext` awaits that close before it sweeps and relaunches.\n   - `acquire` checked the `closed` flag at its top and then awaited the per-profile chain, which can\n     be a whole run long; `ensureContext` never re-checked. A waiter that woke after `closeAll`\n     launched a fresh HEADED browser into a daemon that was shutting down. Shutdown is now final: the\n     check moved into `ensureContext`, and a waiter that wakes after close is refused.\n   - `lease.page()` after release used to open a fresh page nobody would ever close (a step still in\n     flight when the run's release lands). It now refuses by name.\n\nclients/bridge/src/cli/commands/serve.ts\n  installShutdown() - the SIGINT/SIGTERM sequence as ONE testable unit (signal registration injected,\n  so a test never raises a real signal): zeroize secrets, close the socket and surface, AWAIT the\n  browser teardown bounded at 10s, then drop the pidfile and resolve. It used to be a closure inside\n  `serve()` whose ordering nothing could assert, which is how `void profiles.closeAll()` survived.\n\napi/src/automation/browser-session.ts\n  BrowserLease {id, used} - one per call tree, minted per execution PASS, threaded through\n  `RunContext.browserLease` so a sub-automation is a TENANT of its parent's lease rather than a\n  second one queueing behind it. `used` is set by the session on its first frame (not at\n  construction: the engine builds a session for every step of a daemon-connected run).\n  releaseBrowserLease() - the run-end signal, sent once by the owning pass from the engine's run\n  `finally`. Never throws, never silent.\n  DaemonBrowserSession - stamps the leaseId on every frame, heartbeats the lease every 45s from its\n  first step, and stops the heartbeat on dispose(). dispose() no longer ends the lease: a\n  sub-automation's session disposing would have torn the browser down under its parent."
  },
