    "lineHeight": 1.25,
    "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: one new file in `bridge/`, no new module boundary).\n\napi/src/bridge/egress-endpoint.ts (NEW)\n  The validator for a machine's SELF-ASSERTED egress address. A pure function with NO imports -\n  no store, no config module, no seam - so it adds no edge to the tier graph and cannot be a\n  cycle. It sits in `bridge/` because that is where the value ENTERS the system (the `hello`\n  frame) and where its only two writers live.\n    normaliseEgressEndpoint(raw, policy) -> canonical `scheme://host[:port]` | null\n    egressEndpointPolicy()               -> reads EKOA_BRIDGE_ALLOW_PRIVATE_EGRESS (closed default)\n  Two callers inside the module, and deliberately both:\n    bridge/server.ts     the `hello` handler - refuses at INGRESS and warns the operator\n    bridge/registry.ts   `registerPairing` (the write) and `egressCandidatesForOrg` (the read that\n                         selection consumes), so a row written by any other path is re-judged\n                         before it can become a launch option\n  `automation/` still does not import `bridge/` and does not need to: the candidate list it\n  receives through `setEgressCandidateResolver` already carries only authorised addresses, so the\n  one-way tier rule is unchanged by this fix.\n\napi/src/bridge/capability-grants.ts\n  CapabilityGrantDoc gains `egressEndpoint` - the address an `egress.residential` grant authorises.\n  Same module, same store, additive field; `grantCapability` throws CapabilityGrantError when that\n  capability is granted without a usable one.",
    "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: one new file in `bridge/`, no new module boundary).\n\napi/src/bridge/egress-endpoint.ts (NEW)\n  The validator for a machine's SELF-ASSERTED egress address. A pure function with NO imports -\n  no store, no config module, no seam - so it adds no edge to the tier graph and cannot be a\n  cycle. It sits in `bridge/` because that is where the value ENTERS the system (the `hello`\n  frame) and where its only two writers live.\n    normaliseEgressEndpoint(raw, policy) -> canonical `scheme://host[:port]` | null\n    egressEndpointPolicy()               -> reads EKOA_BRIDGE_ALLOW_PRIVATE_EGRESS (closed default)\n  Two callers inside the module, and deliberately both:\n    bridge/server.ts     the `hello` handler - refuses at INGRESS and warns the operator\n    bridge/registry.ts   `registerPairing` (the write) and `egressCandidatesForOrg` (the read that\n                         selection consumes), so a row written by any other path is re-judged\n                         before it can become a launch option\n  `automation/` still does not import `bridge/` and does not need to: the candidate list it\n  receives through `setEgressCandidateResolver` already carries only authorised addresses, so the\n  one-way tier rule is unchanged by this fix.\n\napi/src/bridge/capability-grants.ts\n  CapabilityGrantDoc gains `egressEndpoint` - the address an `egress.residential` grant authorises.\n  Same module, same store, additive field; `grantCapability` throws CapabilityGrantError when that\n  capability is granted without a usable one.",
    "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-19 (P4 ROUND EIGHT: one new file in `bridge/`, no new module boundary).\n\napi/src/bridge/egress-endpoint.ts (NEW)\n  The validator for a machine's SELF-ASSERTED egress address. A pure function with NO imports -\n  no store, no config module, no seam - so it adds no edge to the tier graph and cannot be a\n  cycle. It sits in `bridge/` because that is where the value ENTERS the system (the `hello`\n  frame) and where its only two writers live.\n    normaliseEgressEndpoint(raw, policy) -> canonical `scheme://host[:port]` | null\n    egressEndpointPolicy()               -> reads EKOA_BRIDGE_ALLOW_PRIVATE_EGRESS (closed default)\n  Two callers inside the module, and deliberately both:\n    bridge/server.ts     the `hello` handler - refuses at INGRESS and warns the operator\n    bridge/registry.ts   `registerPairing` (the write) and `egressCandidatesForOrg` (the read that\n                         selection consumes), so a row written by any other path is re-judged\n                         before it can become a launch option\n  `automation/` still does not import `bridge/` and does not need to: the candidate list it\n  receives through `setEgressCandidateResolver` already carries only authorised addresses, so the\n  one-way tier rule is unchanged by this fix.\n\napi/src/bridge/capability-grants.ts\n  CapabilityGrantDoc gains `egressEndpoint` - the address an `egress.residential` grant authorises.\n  Same module, same store, additive field; `grantCapability` throws CapabilityGrantError when that\n  capability is granted without a usable one."
  }
 ]
